  "solution": {
    "text": "In a parallel combination, each branch should have equal potential. But in AC circuit, in a branch,<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></msub><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mn>12</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mtext>R</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><msub><mo>)</mo><mi>rms</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>69</mn></msqrt></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rms</mtext></msub></math><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>&#8756;</mo> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mfenced><mi>peak</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>138</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math